{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{
 "url": "https://www.gerresheimer.com/fileadmin/user_upload/user_upload/landingpages/adlm-2025/Druckvariante_Doppelseite_Diagnostik_Demonstrator_englisch.pdf",
 "title": "PDF document",
 "text": "Diagnostic demonstrator project: Challenges of developing  \ndiagnostic test cartridges for high volume manufacture.  \nA new approach to reduce development times and lower costs.\nDeveloping cost-effective \npoint of care test cartridges\nAuthor:\nNick Smartt\nDevelopment Engineer\n\n\nDeveloping cost-effective point of care  \ntest cartridges\nA selection of injection moulded diagnostic \nproducts developed or produced by Gerresheimer\nBringing a diagnostic test to the market \nin the form of a cartridge-based system \nis not straightforward. Considerable \ndevelopment effort is needed to reach the \nlevel of performance and reliability needed \nfor regulatory approval and market launch. \nAs well as ensuring the biology works reliably \nin a cartridge format, to achieve commercial \nsuccess, the product must be designed for \ncost-effective manufacture in the planned \nproduction volumes.\nWe asked ourselves, can we find a way to \nstreamline the development process and \nreduce the costs and risks associated with \ndeveloping a new diagnostic test cartridge? \nAs well as being a contract manufacturer, \nGerresheimer supports customers with the \ndesign and development of injection moulded \nmedical products for high volume manufacture. \nAn area of growth in recent years has been \ndiagnostic cartridges, where customers have \napproached us to support the development  \nof point of care test systems.\nThe Diagnostics Demonstrator Project \nWith each successful project, similar develop- \nments become faster, more predictable, more \nroutine, easier to plan, and less likely to run \ninto difficulties. We initiated an internal project \nto create our own cartridge, investigating and \nfinding solutions to common challenges, at the \nsame time creating a demonstrator to show \nto potential customers. By including functions \nand features that can be adapted and reused, \nwe aimed to give ourselves a head start when \nwe encounter similar issues in other projects. \nWe noticed development teams working on \ndifferent cartridge projects facing similar \nchallenges. How can liquids be moved without \ngenerating air bubbles or leaving liquid behind? \nHow can channels be manufactured with tight \ncontrol of shape and dimensional tolerances? \nWhat is the best way to seal channels without \nrestricting flow? What materials are most \nsuitable for cartridges, lids, membranes, etc?\nPoint of Care test cartridge\nProduct: Bio Chip Reader\nApplication: Diagnostics\nProduct: Multiplexer\nApplication: Diagnostics\nProduct: Rapid test\nApplication: Microbiology\nProduct: XBC Test Cartridge\nApplication: Diagnostics,  \nHematology\nProduct: Specimen  \ncollection vessel\nApplication: Microbiology\nProduct: Drugwipe\nApplication: Diagnostics\n32\n\nRequirements definition\nIn contrast to projects for external customers, \nhere we had to define for ourselves what \nwe wished to achieve. Targets needed to \nbe realistic, as project resources were not \nunlimited, but shouldn’t be too easy either. \nWe wanted to build know-how, and this \nmeant taking on new challenges. Working with \ncolleagues in Sales, Marketing and Innovation \nManagement, we listed possible features \nand functions. Features without widespread \napplication or ideas requiring excessive \ndevelopment time were rejected, and the \nremaining functions divided into essential  \nand optional requirements.\nFunction Essential Option\nInsert liquid sample into cartridge x\nInsert swab into cartridge x\nLeak-tight connection to cartridge x\nLiquid transport through fluidic channels x\nMix liquids x\nStorage of liquid reagents x\nStorage of lyophilized reagents x\nMagnetic beads x\nFlow control (on-off) valve x\nOne-way valve x\nDistribution valve x\nHeating to 95°C x\nCooling from 95°C to 40°C x\nWindow for fluorescent reading x\nChallenges in Designing PoC Test Cartridges\nEstablished laboratory processes such \nas pipetting, centrifuging and filtering are \nthe building blocks of lab-based assays. \nHowever these seemingly trivial processes \ncan be difficult to implement reliably and cost \neffectively in a cartridge format. The sample \nmust be transported through the cartridge \nwithout leaving liquid behind, entrapping air or \ngenerating gas bubbles that can compromise \nthe accuracy of metering and interfere with \noptical signals.\nHeat transfer can present a challenge, \nespecially as cartridges are typically made \nfrom plastics or other insulating materials.  \nThe sample must quickly be brought up to  \nthe required incubation temperature and,  \nif thermocycling is used for PCR, efficient  \nheat transfer is the key to achieving fast  \ncycle times. \nAn important consideration is how far to go \nwith miniaturising the assay. Smaller reaction \nvolumes reduce the amount of expensive \nreagents needed, as well as allowing a smaller, \npotentially lower cost cartridge. Alternatively, \ndevelopment may be easier and faster if larger \nvolumes are used.\nDesigning a cartridge involves finding \ncompromises between conflicting demands of \nfluidics, heat transfer, optics, materials science \nand injection moulding. A multi-disciplinary \napproach is needed to fully understand the \ntrade-offs and achieve the best solution within \nthe project timescale. Whatever the technical \nbackground of the person taking these design \ndecisions, it is vital that the considerations \nof other technical disciplines are properly \nunderstood and taken into account.\nSome microfluidic techniques were originally \ndeveloped in research labs and are difficult \nto scale up for volume manufacture. Although \nsuch techniques are useful in advancing \nunderstanding and allowing relatively low \ncost test models to be tested and iterated, \nit is important to consider manufacturability \nneeds as the product is developed. Otherwise \navailable development effort may be used \nineffectively, creating solutions that need \nextensive redesign before they can be \nmanufactured cost effectively.\nComplex and expensive single use plastic \nproducts are becoming increasingly \nunacceptable, especially in the Eur\n\n[Content truncated]",
 "truncated": true,
 "budget": "0 of 12 used for this brief"
}"}],"isError":false}

{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{
 "url": "https://www.aama-ntl.org/docs/default-source/state-sop-laws/scope-of-practice-for-medical-assistants-under-texas-law.pdf?sfvrsn=4a9ff775_1",
 "title": "PDF document",
 "text": "Scope of Practice for Medical Assistants under Texas Law 1 \nDonald A. Balasa, JD, MBA; CEO and House Legal Counsel; [redacted email] 2 \nAmerican Association of Medical Assistants (AAMA) 3 \nAugust 2022 4 \n 5 \nMedical assistants continue to be in high demand throughout the United States.  Medical assisting scope 6 \nof practice is determined primarily by state law.  This paper will explain the scope of practice for medical 7 \nassistants under Texas law. 8 \n 9 \nPhysician delegation 10 \nAs is the case under the laws of many states, Texas law does not refer to “medical assistants” by name.  11 \nMedical assistants are classified as “qualified and properly trained persons” under the Texas Medical 12 \nPractice Act.  Note the following excerpt from this statute: 13 \nOCCUPATIONS CODE 14 \nTITLE 3. HEALTH PROFESSIONS 15 \nSUBTITLE B. PHYSICIANS 16 \nCHAPTER 157. AUTHORITY OF PHYSICIAN TO DELEGATE CERTAIN MEDICAL ACTS 17 \nSUBCHAPTER A. GENERAL PROVISIONS            18 \nSec. 157.001.  GENERAL AUTHORITY OF PHYSICIAN TO DELEGATE.  (a)  A physician may 19 \ndelegate to a qualified and properly trained person acting under the physician's supervision any 20 \nmedical act that a reasonable and prudent physician would find within the scope of sound medical 21 \njudgment to delegate if, in the opinion of the delegating physician: 22 \n(1)  the act: 23 \n(A)  can be properly and safely performed by the person to whom the medical act is delegated; 24 \n(B)  is performed in its customary manner; and 25 \n(C)  is not in violation of any other statute; and 26 \n(2)  the person to whom the delegation is made does not represent to the public that the person is 27 \nauthorized to practice medicine. 28 \n(b)  The delegating physician remains responsible for the medical acts of the person performing the 29 \ndelegated medical acts. 30 \n(c)  The board [Texas Medical Board] may determine whether: 31 \n(1)  an act constitutes the practice of medicine, not inconsistent with this chapter; and 32 \n(2)  a medical act may be properly or safely delegated by physicians. 33 \nActs 1999, 76th Leg., ch. 388, Sec. 1, eff. Sept. 1, 1999.               34 \nSec. 157.002.  GENERAL DELEGATION OF ADMINISTRATION AND PROVISION OF 35 \nDANGEROUS DRUGS.  (a)  In this section: 36 \n(1)  \"Administering\" means the direct application of a drug to the body of a patient by injection, 37 \ninhalation, ingestion, or any other means. 38 \n(2)  \"Provision\" means the supply of one or more unit doses of a drug, medicine, or dangerous drug. 39 \n(b)  A physician may delegate to any qualified and properly trained person acting under the physician's 40 \nsupervision the act of administering or providing dangerous drugs in the physician's office, as ordered by 41 \nthe physician, that are used or required to meet the immediate needs of the physician's patients.  The 42 \nadministration or provision of the dangerous drugs must be performed in compliance with laws relating to 43 \nthe practice of medicine and state and federal laws relating to those dangerous drugs. 44 \n…… 45 \n(e)  The administration or provision of the drugs may be delegated through a physician's order, a standing 46 \nmedical order, a standing delegation order, or another order defined by the board. 47 \n…… 48 \nSec. 157.005.  PERFORMANCE OF DELEGATED ACT NOT PRACTICING WITHOUT MEDICAL 49 \nLICENSE.  A person to whom a physician delegates the performance of a medical act is not considered to 50 \nbe practicing medicine without a license by performing the medical act unless the person acts with 51 \nknowledge that the delegation and the action taken under the delegation is a violation of this subtitle. 52 \nActs 1999, 76th Leg., ch. 388, Sec. 1, eff. Sept. 1, 1999. 53 \n\nSec. 157.006.  LIMITATION ON BOARD RULES REGARDING DELEGATION.  The board shall 54 \npromote a physician's exercise of professional judgment to decide which medical acts may be safely 55 \ndelegated by not adopting rules containing, except as absolutely necessary, global prohibitions or 56 \nrestrictions on the delegation of medical acts. 57 \nActs 1999, 76th Leg., ch. 388, Sec. 1, eff. Sept. 1, 1999. 58 \n 59 \nDelegation of IV tasks 60 \nThe Texas Medical Board was asked the following question: 61 \nSpecifically, my question is whether the Texas Medical Practice Act and the regulations and 62 \npolicies of the Texas Medical Board permit physicians to delegate to medical assistants the 63 \ninitiating and discontinuing of an IV. 64 \nThe Texas Medical Board’s response was as follows: 65 \nA physician may delegate to a qualified and properly trained person if they see fit.  Please review 66 \nMedical Practice Act Chapter 157 AUTHORITY OF PHYSICIAN TO DELEGATE CERTAIN 67 \nMEDICAL ACTS. 68 \nThis response indicates that Texas law permits physicians to delegate to “qualified and properly trained 69 \npersons” such as medical assistants the performing of IV tasks. 70 \n 71 \nNurse delegation to medical assistants 72 \nMedical assistants are classified as “unlicensed persons” under the Texas nursing law.  The following 73 \napplies to Advanced Practice Registered Nurses (APRNs) as well as to RNs. Note the following from the 74 \nTexas Board of Nursing regulations: 75 \n§224.6. General Criteria for Delegation. 76 \nThe following standards must be met before the RN delegates nursing tasks to unlicensed persons. These 77 \ncriteria apply to all instances of RN delegation. Additional criteria, if appropriate to the particular task 78 \nbeing delegated, may also be found in §224.8(b)(1) of this title (relating to Discretionary Delegation 79 \nTasks).  80 \n(1) The RN must make an assessment of the client’s nursing care needs. The RN should, when the client’s 81 \nstatus allows, consult with the client, and when appropriate the client’s family and/or significant other(s), 82 \nto identify the client’s nursing needs prior to delegating nursing tasks.  83 \n(2) The nursing task must be one that a reasonable and prudent RN would find is within the scope of 84 \nsound nursing judgment to delegate. The RN should consider the five rights of delegation: the right task, 85 \nthe right person to whom the delegation is made, the right circumstances, the right direction and 86 \ncommunication by the RN, and the right supervision as determined by the RN.  87 \n(3) The nursing task must be one that, in the opinion of the delegating RN, can be properly and safely 88 \nperformed by the unlicensed person involved without jeopardizing the client’s welfare.  89 \n(4) The nursing task must not require the unlicensed person to exercise professional nursing judgment; 90 \nhowever, the unlicensed person may take any action that a reasonable, prudent non-health care 91 \nprofessional would take in an emergency situation.  92 \n(5) The unlicensed person to whom the nursing task is delegated must be adequately identified. The 93 \nidentification may be by individual or, if appropriate, by training, education, and/or certification/permit of 94 \nthe unlicensed person.  95 \n(6) The RN shall have either instructed the unlicensed person in the delegated task, or verified the 96 \nunlicensed person’s competency to perform the nursing task. The verification of competence may be done 97 \nby the RN making the decision to delegate or, if appropriate, by training, education, experience and/or 98 \ncertification/permit of the unlicensed person.  99 \n(7) The RN shall adequately supervise the performance of the delegated nursing task in accordance with 100 \nthe requirements of §224.7 of this title (relating to Supervision).  101 \n(8) If the delegation continues over time, the RN shall periodically evaluate, review, and when a change 102 \nin condition occurs reevaluate the delegation of tasks. For example, the evaluation would be appropriate 103 \nwhen the client’s Nursing Care Plan is reviewed and revised. The RN’s evaluation of a delegated task(s) 104 \nwill be incorporated into the client’s Nursing Care Plan. 105",
