  "why": "This single measure decides the case. At 1.22 panels a day the three-year cartridge gross profit of $15,621 does not repay the $25,600-51,200 cost of winning a placement, and $78.5M of 2029 revenue needs 5,150 sites rather than 300. At 6 or more, the LTV:CAC clears 3:1, the 300-site plan produces $30-60M and a $300M+ exit comes back into view. Every other variable — clearance date, waiver, cartridge cost, fee schedule — moves the timing or the margin; utilisation sets the level, and no evidence in the file yet shows it at a billing-enabled site."
 },
 "modules": {
  "analogs": "LumiraDx (cautionary, 2021-2024): listed at a $3.0B de-SPAC valuation after cutting the deal from $5.0B and sold its point-of-care technology to Roche for $295M after lenders took control — a multi-assay platform with revenue still went for a fifth of its listing value. Cue Health (cautionary, 2021-2024): raised $480M+ and reached a $2.3B valuation, then filed Chapter 7 in 2024 after an FDA warning letter on unauthorised device changes and lot-release failures — the closest precedent to Vantelle's quality-system gap. QuidelOrtho-LEX Diagnostics (pricing, 2026): $100M cash for a platform already cleared and CLIA-waived, the current clearing price for the asset Vantelle will hold in 2028. Truvian Sciences (cautionary, 2015-2026): raised $294M, cleared 12 analytes by 2026-01-12 and remains private eleven years after founding — the base rate for finger-stick platforms reaching commercial scale late and at a discount. The decision rule: a cleared, waived, revenue-generating point-of-care platform transacts at $100-300M in this market, so entry must leave room for a 3x return below that band.",
  "capital_efficiency": "$51.5M of equity and debt has produced $2.6M of FY2025 revenue, $0.05 of revenue per dollar raised and a burn multiple of 8.3x in FY2025, 4.2x in FY2026E and 15.6x excluding the $2.4M stocking order, against a Series B median near 1.4x. The 2029 profit assumes $46.7M of operating cost carrying $78.5M of revenue; breakeven at the 71% target margin needs $65.8M of revenue, 884 analyzers at 2,400 panels a year, twice the sites targeted for end-2027. Two million cartridges, the volume behind the $7.50 cost, needs 5,500 analyzers at observed use; the 402 analyzers behind 300 sites produce 147,000 cartridges, 7% of it, at a disclosed cost of $16.20 and a 48% margin. Capital needed is $40M on the company's own plan and $55-70M in the stress case, against a $30M round.",
  "position_sizing": "Private company: do not lead at $140M pre-money. If Meridian invests, cap the entry at $70M pre-money, where $15M buys about 17.6% and a 3x return needs a $301M exit, and tranche it $7.5M at close and $7.5M on 510(k) clearance with a waiver milestone attached. Reserve one times the initial cheque ($15M) for the follow-on the plan itself requires, for a $30M total exposure. The bear case is a return of capital, not a loss of it: at a $50-100M sale the $36.3M senior preference is covered and the Series B recovers about 1.0-1.2x, with the Corvia put taking roughly $9.4M ahead of it; common equity is near zero. A full loss needs no sale at all or a price below the preference stack. The base case at 1.0-1.5x is below the venture hurdle; only the bull case, at about 2.4x, clears it."
