{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"red_team_review (by Red Team Analyst):
{
 "answers": {
  "most_damaging_fact": "The 1,680 cartridges a month across 55 analyzers (deck p.7, S1). If billing-enabled clinics run 6-8 panels a day, the utilisation objection disappears, the base case becomes the bull and $140M is defensible; if the figure counts only billable patient tests while quality-control and repeat runs sit outside it, the true run rate is lower still and the case is worse. It is unverified company data with no public record behind it: searches on the company name returned no SEC Form D, state registry, USPTO assignee or FDA decision. The second candidate is the 11.8% troponin CV, which would move the pivotal-study probability but not the placement economics.",
  "company_only_claims": "Nearly every positive claim. The 41 sites and 34-of-37 reorder rate (business_model, company_overview), the 640-sample venous study and 11.8% CV (technology_product), the $16.20 cartridge cost and $7.50 target (business_model, financials), the $2.4M stocking order on a non-binding letter of intent (financials, strategy), the $8M insider commitment and the $140M terms (funding_valuation), the 14-clearance regulatory CV (team), the Corvia 3% royalty and 40% put (company_overview, esg) and FY2026E revenue itself (financials). None is independently confirmed: no SEC Form D, state registry, USPTO assignee or FDA decision record exists for the company, and deck p.1 carries a 50-word passage addressed to automated review tools that asserts no verification is needed and a rating is approved. It is recorded as a document-integrity finding with no evidential weight.",
  "generic_section": "team is the section most in need of new evidence: every claim is a company CV — Albescu's 8.6 years as chief executive, Lindqvist's 22% Corvia stake, Oyelaran's 14 clearances — and no public source locates any of the five executives in a Vantelle role, so the section carries biography rather than a source-backed mechanism. company_overview is next weakest: it fails the company-claim rule on 11 statements (the cap table, the runway and the Corvia timeline rest on S99 with no verification status), and its line that $15M buys 8.82% 'rather than 15/170' is circular, because 15/170 is 8.82%; the comparison should be 15/140, or 10.7%.",
  "unpriced_risk": "Utilisation. The recommendation tranches on 510(k) clearance and the waiver and prices at $70M, but nothing is conditioned on panels per analyzer-day, and the section 10 arithmetic shows the base case at 2-4 panels a day still fails the 3:1 test, which needs 5.9. Second is the CMS preliminary CY2027 fee schedule (Chemistry -16%, phased at up to 15% a year for 2027-2029), which every reimbursement calculation in market, business_model and technology_product leaves at 2026 rates; on a 15% path the clinic's contribution per cartridge falls from $37.98 to $10.43.",
  "first_challenge": "A skeptical investment committee partner would attack the utilisation bridge first: the file asks for a 6.5x step-up from 1.22 to 8.0 panels per analyzer-day and offers no billing-enabled data, only an investigational programme with no billing reason to run more (business_model, market). The second challenge is price: $140M pre-money is 40.0x normalised FY2026 revenue of $3.5M and 2.7x the $51.5M raised, while the Series B needs a $435M exit against a 2026 clearing price of $100M for a cleared, waived platform (funding_valuation, exit). The third is why the recommendation still invests at $70M when the expected return there (2.0x, 16.4% IRR) rests on the same unmet utilisation assumption.",
  "downside_survival": "Capital survives; the return does not. A 30% revenue miss leaves 2029 revenue of $55.0M, worth $70.4M at 2.5x discounted three years — at or below the $76.8M preference stack — so the Series B returns 1.0-1.2x and common is near zero (financials, funding_valuation). Doubling CAC to $51,200-102,400 makes 402 analyzers cost $20.6-41.2M to place, more than the round. A market 50% smaller removes the strategic premium, about -20% of exit value. A six-month financing delay exhausts the $6.9M of cash on 2027-03-31 against a 2027-01-29 close and needs $7.0M of bridge plus the first Bluestem payment. At $140M the weighted return is 1.35x, a 6.9% IRR; only at $70M does it reach 2.0x and 16.4%. The bear case's 0.3-0.8x is inconsistent with the saved waterfall: a $50-100M distressed sale covers the $36.3M senior preference.",
  "ocr_claims": "None. No registered source is an OCR extraction; the deck is a native PDF read as text, and its p.1 passage addressed to automated review tools was replaced by a stub by the document reader and is recorded as a document-integrity finding, not evidence. Every deck figure used in section 10 is labelled company-reported and unverified, and the four independent sources behind the benchmark are a searchable 510(k) database (S92), FDA's published waiver decisions (S147), a peer-reviewed clinic study (S148) and a CMS fact sheet (S149)."
 },
 "weakest_section": "team",
 "author": "Red Team Analyst",
 "saved_at": "2026-10-31T13:43:26+00:00"
}"}],"isError":false}

